  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nIncreasing cloud cover and warm advection overnight will keep\nlow temps very mild for this time of year. Temps Fri morning may\nactually be closer to average highs this time of year. That will\nlead to another well above normal day for most of the area Fri.\nThe one exception will be across the mtns, where clouds and\nshowers will arrive early enough to cap daytime heating. Here I\nblended in hi-res guidance to the NBM to knock those readings\nback into the 60s. Otherwise the high floor start to the day\nwill allow temps to run well into the 70s again with some 80s\npossible. The rain showers will not be much to write home about\nas the front moves thru the region. Most areas should remain\nless than a tenth of an inch. Otherwise any rain will make\nvalley/radiation fog more likely Fri night as we clear out.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA polar air mass brings the coldest overnight temperatures so \nfar this season early next week. Many locations south of the \nmountains may see their first hard freeze. \n  \nDISCUSSION: Strong high pressure slides through the area this \nweekend bringing dry and cooler weather. The front slides \nthrough during the day Saturday so temperatures in areas north \nof the mountains top out in the upper 50s and low 60s. From the \nfoothills south, there should be enough time to warm into the \nmid to upper 60s. The front should be through Saturday night so \njust about everyone likely wakes up to temperatures in the 30s \nSunday morning. Clouds then build across the region early on \nSunday ahead of an approaching trough so after a chilly morning \ntemperatures aren't expected to rebound much and likely only \nclimb into the low to mid- 60s areawide. On the other hand, \nclouds stay abundant Sunday night only allowing temperatures to \nfall into the upper 30s and 40s.\n\nVertically stacked low pressure moves through Quebec on Monday \nand dragging a front through our region. Ensembles are not \ncurrently very excited about this being a worthwhile \nprecipitation event as probabilities of greater than a tenth of \nan inch decrease to 10 to 30 percent outside of the mountains. \nThe bigger weather story is going to be the cold dry airmass \nthat fills in behind this front. In fact, any showers that \nlinger in the mountains Monday night may transition to light \nsnow. High temperatures Tuesday look to only reach the upper 40s\nto mid-50s from north to south, and with a tightening pressure \ngradient, breezy winds may make it feel even colder. Long range \nensembles suggest dewpoints drop into the upper 20s and low 30s \nbehind the front, and with 850 mb temperatures also -5 to 0 C \nthis seems like a reasonable floor for surface temperatures \nTuesday and Wednesday morning. The current NBM is capturing this\nreasonably well with upper 20s and low 30s across the mountains\nand foothills, and mid to upper 30s for the coastal plain. I \nwouldn't be surprised to see those temperatures on the coastal \nplain come down as we get into the hi-res window as it looks \nlike a favorable setup for radiational cooling. These zones are \nalso still eligible for Frost/Freeze products, so this will have\nto be watched closely.\n\nTemperatures become more seasonable again Wednesday and \nThursday night as high pressure exits to the east and we see \nsouthwesterly winds through the column.",
